In the June 2024 release, the defaulted parameter {\f2 initialState}  for the {\f2 CreateUploadBuffer}  function in {\i BufferHelpers}  was removed. Per the {\b DirectX} 12 validation layer, the only valid initial state for an upload buffer is {\f2 D3D12_RESOURCE_STATE_GENERIC_READ} .\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Starting with the February 2023 release, the Mouse class implementation of relative mouse movement was updated to accumulate changes between calls to {\f2 GetState} . By default, each time you call {\f2 GetState}  the deltas are reset which works for scenarios where you use relative movement but only call the method once per frame. If you call it more than once per frame, then add an explicit call to {\f2 EndOfInputFrame}  to use an explicit reset model instead.\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
As of the September 2022 release, the library makes use of C++11 inline namespaces for differing types that have the same names in the {\b DirectX} 11 and {\b DirectX} 12 version of the {\i {\b DirectX} Tool Kit} . This provides a link-unique name such as {\f2 {\b DirectX::DX12::SpriteBatch}}  that will appear in linker output messages. In most use cases, however, there is no need to add explicit {\f2 DX12}  namespace resolution in client code.\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Starting with the June 2021 release, this library builds the HLSL shaders with Shader Model 6 via DXC. See {\f2 this wiki page} for more information. The Microsoft GDK projects have always used Shader Model 6.\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Starting with the June 2020 release, this library makes use of {\f2 typed enum bitmask flags} per the recommendation of the {\i C++ Standard}  section {\i 17.5.2.1.3 Bitmask types} . This may have {\i breaking change}  impacts to client code:{
\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u9702\tab}\ilvl1\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx720\li720\ri0\lin720\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
You cannot pass the {\f2 0}  literal as your flags value. Instead you must make use of the appropriate default enum value: {\f2 AudioEngine_Default} , {\f2 SoundEffectInstance_Default} , {\f2 ModelLoader_Clockwise} , {\f2 DDS_LOADER_DEFAULT} , or {\f2 WIC_LOADER_DEFAULT} .\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u9702\tab}\ilvl1\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx720\li720\ri0\lin720\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Use the enum type instead of {\f2 DWORD}  if building up flags values locally with bitmask operations. For example, {\f2 WIC_LOADER_FLAGS flags = WIC_LOADER_DEFAULT; if (...) flags |= WIC_LOADER_FORCE_SRGB;} \par}
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
The UWP projects and the Win10 classic desktop project include configurations for the ARM64 platform. {\b Building} these requires installing the ARM64 toolset.\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
When using clang/LLVM for the ARM64 platform, the Windows 11 SDK ({\f2 22000}) or later is required.\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
The {\f2 CompileShaders.cmd}  script must have Windows-style (CRLF) line-endings. If it is changed to Linux-style (LF) line-endings, it can fail to build all the required shaders.\par}
\par
